WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.300
All right.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.100
Let's get started.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.360
So today, we're going to
continue our discussion

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.437
about MPC.

00:00:10.437 --> 00:00:11.020
In particular.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.960
We're going to wrap
up our discussion

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.280
about persistent feasibility
as one of the key properties

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.840
we want to imbue in our
MPC control pipeline.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.620
And then we'll switch to
the topic of stability.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:28.600
So not only we want to make sure
that MPC loop has a feasible

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.640
solution at each point
in time, but we also

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.280
want to make sure
that whatever action

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.240
we optimize for does
something useful.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.160
In our problem
setting, useful means

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.480
we are stabilizing the system
that is converging to 0.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.120
We'll then wrap
up the discussion

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:50.200
with a couple of topics that
are of practical relevance,

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.680
specifically how some
of the optimization

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.440
could actually be computed
offline as opposed to online.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.830
That was actually a pretty big
deal about 10 years ago where

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.550
hardware was not
particularly advanced yet,

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.710
and optimization solvers were
not particularly advanced yet.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:09.350
So offloading part of
optimization offline

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.590
was a big deal
because it relieved

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:13.310
what you had to do online.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.630
So basically, you
minimize delays.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.750
And then we're going to talk
about the application of MPC

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.910
to a slightly more
general setting, which is

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.950
that of a trajectory tracking.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.630
And I will show you how
the MPC formulation has

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:28.870
to be modified a little
bit to ensure that you have

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:30.450
a good tracking performance.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.970
Interestingly enough, sometimes
people in industry get it wrong.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:37.590
So I want to make sure that you
are not one of those people.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:38.090
All right.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.750
So last time, we introduced
the concept of MPC.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:46.030
It's a bit dark here.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:50.030
Let's leave it at this before
the entire setup explodes.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.390
So we talked about MPC.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:55.910
I introduced the concept.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:59.870
I introduced a specific
problem set up,

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:03.110
which is that of controlling
a linear time varying system.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.230
Such were two
polyhedral constraints.

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:10.289
We talked about the problem
persistent feasibility

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:11.030
and stability.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.650
We introduced some concepts,
in particular the concept

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.450
of a one-step controllable
set, the concept

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.610
of controlling variance
set, the concept

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:23.930
of a positive environment set,
the concept of feasibility set.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.690
And then we stated
the lemma, which

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:29.050
reads as if set x1,
which is essentially

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.690
truncated feasibility
set, where we look

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:38.130
at feasible state control
pairs starting from stage

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.930
1 as opposed to 0.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:44.730
Well, if this set x1 is control
invariant set for the system

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.690
under consideration,
then the MPC law

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.770
is persistently
feasible, which means

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.410
that at each planning
cycle, we have

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:53.530
at least one feasible solution.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:56.490
As I mentioned last
time, this result per se

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:00.120
is not particularly
useful, because x1 is not

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.520
one of the tuning knobs that
you can manipulate in order

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.960
to tune your MPC.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.280
But we'll see it's a stepping
stone for the theorem

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.840
that I'm going to state in a few
minutes that actually relates

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.840
the concept of
persistent feasibility

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.640
to one of our tuning knobs.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.960
The t is actually
the terminal set, xf,

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.880
that we use as one of the
constraints in our MPC

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.160
formulation.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.920
With that context, let's
prove this feasibility lemma.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:36.600
The proof is not
particularly interesting,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.440
but at least it stress
tests your understanding

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:45.920
of some of the concepts
we have discussed so far.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.880
So remember that we
want to show that--

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.520
sorry.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:57.190
If x1 is control invariant
set, then the MPC low

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:58.690
is persistently feasible.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:01.070
So once we transition
to the new state

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.130
from the perspective
of the new state x1,

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:06.990
we still have a control that is
feasible over the optimization

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.190
horizon.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:12.070
So the proof is as follows.

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:18.230
Remember the definition of
a one-step controllable set.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:23.790
So the one-step controllable
set to x1 or previous of x1

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.783
is defined as the set of states.

00:04:25.783 --> 00:04:27.950
That's just the definition
that we stated last time.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:31.150
It's defined as a set
of states in Rn, such

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.630
that there exists a
control, a feasible control,

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:40.990
such that Ax plus Bu
transitions into the set x1.

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:46.510
So that's the definition
of a one-step control set.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:52.110
Now, since x1 is
control invariant,

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.150
again, by definition
of control invariance

00:04:54.150 --> 00:05:00.010
for all states within x1, there
exists a feasible control such

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:05.810
that Ax plus Bu is in X1.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:10.650
Now, if we compare
the two definitions,

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:18.610
we see that x1, a point
that is in x1 also

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:22.270
belongs to the previous of x1.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.150
So the one-step controllable
set is the same property.

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:32.750
But the point that is in x1
also has to be in capital X,

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:36.410
by the definition of x1.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:40.770
X1 is first of all the set of
states that are feasible so

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:49.450
that are in x, which
means that the set

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:55.240
x1 has to be a subset of the
intersection between the set x.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:01.200
This is trivial and
the previous of x1.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.260
Does it make sense?

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:05.240
It's a bit of a
mouthful, but it just

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.840
follows from the definitions.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:16.640
Now, let's go back one step and
now reason in terms set X of 0.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.040
So this is the set
of states, x of 0,

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.440
such that there is a
sequence of controls,

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:27.400
such that the states
generated under the dynamics

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:33.360
are produced states
that are feasible.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.480
Remember the definition of X0.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:40.040
X0 is the set of feasible
states such that there exists

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:43.480
a sequence of controls,
feasible controls,

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:48.060
such that under the
dynamics evolution,

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.120
the generated states are
also feasible and satisfy

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.030
the terminal condition.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:56.710
Now, I can rewrite
the definition of X0

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:01.830
as the set of
feasible states such

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:06.310
that there is a feasible control
such that X1 belongs to capital

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:09.830
X1 because capital X1 subsumes.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:11.370
What is the definition of an x1?

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:13.718
Previous slide.

00:07:13.718 --> 00:07:15.510
The fact that there is
a truncated sequence

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:18.410
of controls that leads to a
feasible state trajectory.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:39.150
Now, again, let's compare this
definition to the definition

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.110
here.

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.270
So they are identical.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:44.830
The only difference
is that in here, I'm

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.350
imposing these additional
constraints so that the state

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:49.230
has to belong to X.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:54.010
So X0 is really the
intersection of capital X

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:58.570
and the one-step
controllable set to X1.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:00.690
But then we put
together the two facts,

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:05.370
and we see that X1
is subset of X0.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:10.890
Every state that belongs to X1
is also a state belongs to X0.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:11.950
Does this make sense?

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:13.650
It's mostly basically
pattern-matching

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.230
the different definitions.

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:17.030
So there is no particular
cleverness here.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:17.770
Yes.

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:23.990
So in the previous example, it's
not necessarily a subset of X,

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:24.490
because--

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:30.610
No, no, because of
how we define it here.

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:34.690
It's any state that
transitions us into X1.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:36.770
It could also be a
state that is not

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:44.370
feasible in terms set
membership to capital X. OK.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:48.070
So now there is a slightly
more interesting part.

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:50.800
Actually, this entire
proof is quite boring.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:56.560
Take some state that belongs
to X of 0, some initial state.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.160
And let U star 0 be the solution
to the finite-time optimization

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:04.180
problem, MPC
optimization problem.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:09.320
Let U star 0 be in the first
control in this sequence.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:11.840
Then under the
control, you start 0.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:17.480
Under the MPC control, we
transition to a new state X1.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:19.720
Since you U star 0
is clearly feasible,

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:26.780
X1 belongs to capital X1 because
it's part of the MPC trajectory.

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:28.960
So by definition, it has
to satisfy all the state

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:32.120
constraints under the
dynamics evolution.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.960
And so it belongs to X1.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.720
But we just prove
that set X1 is subset

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:42.400
of X0, which means that
set X1 belongs actually

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:47.480
to set X of 0, which means
that the new MPC optimization

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.460
problem is indeed feasible.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:51.860
And then you can recursively
apply this property

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:54.540
through time.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:56.780
So rewinding all
of this, we wanted

00:09:56.780 --> 00:10:01.180
to prove that under the MPC
control law, the new state

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:09.460
X of 1, X1 belongs to X0, the
set of feasible states for MPC.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:12.380
To do so, we
manipulated a little bit

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:15.180
the definition of a
one-step controllable set

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:19.360
and the notion of
control invariance.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:30.060
And we finally proved
that if set X1 is

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.100
control invariant,
then the problem,

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.860
the MPC problem is
persistently feasible.

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:35.840
Does it make sense?

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:38.260
Again, not a particularly
exciting result, not

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:41.380
also particularly useful, but
it's a good stress test for you

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:45.380
to see whether you
understand the notion

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:47.910
of a one-step controllable
set, control invariant set,

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:48.438
and so on.

00:10:48.438 --> 00:10:50.730
In the final exam, there will
be have one question that

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:55.770
will-- well, I will ask you what
is say a control invariant set.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.270
OK.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:09.530
Anyway-- so say that the
horizon is equal to 1.

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:13.090
Then we can just
choose the terminal

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:14.910
set to be controlling violence.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:18.750
And by the previous lemma, we
know that if that is the case,

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:26.690
then the MPC problem is feasible
at all replanning stages.

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:29.130
But of course, in general,
the optimization horizon

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:30.410
is greater than 1.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:33.210
So how can we extend
these results to the cases

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:37.810
whereby we have a general
optimization horizon capital N?

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.310
So that's what we're
going to do next.

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:45.110
Any questions in the meantime?

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:53.940
So now we get to the
more interesting part.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.620
So building on the lemma
that I just showed,

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:03.000
it says that if X1 is
controlled invariant,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:06.120
then the MPC control law
is persistently feasible.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:09.040
Now, we can prove a
more powerful theorem

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:11.280
that reads as follows.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.760
If the set X of f,
which is a reminder,

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:19.640
is the set through which we
specify a terminal constraint

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.900
for the MPC
optimization problem,

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:24.160
it's something that we decide.

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:29.440
And if set X of f is a control
invariant set for the system

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:35.880
under study, then the MPC
law is persistently feasible.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:37.160
That's interesting.

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.400
Let's try to understand
what is this saying.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:44.120
What is a trivial control
invariant set for this system?

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:46.420
Meaning a set that
if you are there,

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:49.080
there exists a control that
allows you to stay there.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:56.800
By any mathematical equation, if
you are in doubt, always say 0.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:59.340
I mean, typically 0
is the best guess.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:00.520
In this case, it's correct.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:05.020
So the origin 0 is indeed
a control invariant set.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:09.820
If your state is X
equals 0, assuming

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:13.340
that U equals 0 is an allowable
control, which is typically

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:17.580
true, I mean, doing nothing
is typically allowed.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:22.180
Under linear dynamics, if you
are at 0 and you use Control 0,

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:26.740
your next state is 0, which
means that the origin, you

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:29.860
have a set with only one
point, but still it is a set.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:33.220
The origin is indeed a
control invariant set.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:34.960
And intuitively,
what does it say?

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:43.140
It's saying that if we just
target the origin in N steps

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:46.370
and then I shrink
the horizon by 1,

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:50.810
there is always an admissible
control sequence that

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:53.170
is the concatenation
of the previous control

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:56.250
sequence and then the fact
that with the last control

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:57.230
we just say at 0.

00:13:57.230 --> 00:14:00.410
So you can just trivially
continue your control sequence

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:03.290
to have a full and
step control sequence.

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:06.030
So we're going to
prove the theorem,

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:09.890
but the intuition
is quite obvious.

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:13.450
That said, for a
general system, you

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:15.770
might have different
control invariant set.

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:17.110
0 is just an option.

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:19.530
But actually, you could
have a larger set,

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:21.630
which is the control
invariant for the system.

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:25.490
We'll see later how to
compute those larger sets.

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:31.090
So do you think that the origin
is always the best choice?

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:34.770
So constraining your system
to reach the origin in N step

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.530
as part of your MPC
control law, you

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:39.890
think that this is always
the smart thing to do?

00:14:39.890 --> 00:14:41.790
It guarantees you
persistent feasibility.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:42.620
So that's great.

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:50.400
Remember that
typically, you're not

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:54.800
just reasoning in terms of
controlling the state to 0,

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.520
but you also want to be
very parsimonious in terms

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:00.280
of how much control you spend.

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:02.600
So oftentimes,
asking your system

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:07.080
to reach the origin in N steps
as part of your MPC optimization

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.060
problem can be very limiting.

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:11.160
So basically, you
are asking-- so

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:13.380
of all the control
sequences you could apply,

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:15.280
you're only optimizing
over those that

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.160
in N step new to the origin.

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:20.120
It's typically those that
require a lot of control effort.

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:23.400
So yes, you have
persistent feasibility,

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:27.000
but now, you might control
your system very suboptimally,

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:30.480
meaning with a lot of
control expenditure.

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:35.140
So that's why in general,
the terminal constraint--

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:37.960
it is something that you
artificially add to your MPC

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:40.160
problem to ensure
persistent feasibility--

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:43.020
should be as relaxed as possible
so that you don't overly

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:44.320
constrain your system.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:46.660
Does that make sense?

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:52.220
Just trying to give you
both mathematical backing

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:56.020
for what I'm saying, but also
some practical considerations.

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:58.160
And this is stuff that
people do for real.

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:02.100
So it's constantly a little
bit of a design choice

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.540
how to properly set X of f.

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:09.740
So now, let's
prove this theorem.

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:13.700
So the proof is as follows.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:18.020
So we prove that if X of f
is control invariant, then

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:24.580
XN minus 1, the truncated set,
where your horizon is only 1,

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:27.860
is also going to be
control invariant.

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:29.820
And then if XN minus 1
is control invariant.

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:31.540
XN minus 2 is control invariant.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:34.920
All the way to x1,
and once we hit x1,

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:37.282
we know from the
previous lemma that if x1

00:16:37.282 --> 00:16:39.990
is controlling violent, then the
system is persistently feasible.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:42.410
So we can invoke that
lemma to conclude

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:45.730
the proof of the theorem.

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:47.950
So let's start with
the first step.

00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:52.170
So if Xf-- we want to prove
that if Xf is control invariant,

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:56.070
XN minus 1 is control
invariant too.

00:16:56.070 --> 00:17:00.410
XN minus 1 is defined as usual.

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:04.010
Now it's just a
truncated feasibility

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:07.690
set where we only reason in
terms of a one-step horizon.

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:11.490
It's the set of states, feasible
states for which there exists

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:16.089
a feasible control such that
under the system dynamics,

00:17:16.089 --> 00:17:19.609
we have a sequence of
states that is feasible.

00:17:19.609 --> 00:17:25.849
In this case, the last state
needs to satisfy the terminal

00:17:25.849 --> 00:17:26.589
constraint.

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:44.120
Now, because of the terminal
constraint, we know that--

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:47.160
remember that we want to show
that this set is controlled

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:48.200
invariant.

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:52.960
So the question
is for any state,

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:56.840
any vector within
the state, is there

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:00.920
a control input that allows us
to transition back into this

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:04.360
set under the system dynamics?

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:06.040
So that's what we want to prove.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:15.040
Now, what we know is that by
the definition of XN minus 1,

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:20.960
there exists a control such
that XN minus 1 plus B1

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:24.660
is equal to XN, which belongs
to the terminal set X of f.

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:29.760
I mean this is by
essential definition of x1.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:36.840
Now, the question is does
XN which belongs to Xf also

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:38.820
belong to X1?

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:42.160
If that is true, that means
that X1 is control invariant.

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:44.940
I found actually a
control that allows

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:48.820
me to transition back into X1.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:52.620
But for now, the only thing
that I know is that X of N

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:55.380
belongs to Xf.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:19:00.900
But from the assumption of the
theorem, we do know that X of f

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:04.260
is a control
invariant set, which

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:11.300
means that there exists a U
such that Ax plus Bu belongs

00:19:11.300 --> 00:19:14.940
to X of f.

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:17.980
But isn't that the
definition of the--

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:22.540
defines the membership
of a point to XN minus 1.

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:26.140
So we have just proved
that XN minus-- that XN

00:19:26.140 --> 00:19:29.940
indeed belongs to
capital set XN minus 1.

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:34.500
Don't be mixed up
with the subscript X--

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:37.430
XN minus 1 is just a
definition of a set.

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:40.010
Is it a one-step feasible set?

00:19:40.010 --> 00:19:42.450
So the fact that XN
belongs to X minus 1

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:45.170
doesn't tell you nothing in
terms of the temporal coherency

00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:47.690
between the subscripts.

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:54.810
So we have just proven that X of
N belongs to capital XN minus 1.

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:56.810
So we have just
proved that X minus 1

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:58.730
is indeed in control invariant.

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:03.530
But then you can apply this
argument recursively until--

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:06.090
to prove that X minus 2 is
control invariant, x minus 3

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:08.570
is control invariant,
all the way to X1.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:11.650
At that point, we invoke
the feasibility lemma

00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:13.130
from the previous
lecture, and then

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:17.230
we can state the claim, that
if Xf is controlled invariant,

00:20:17.230 --> 00:20:21.530
then MPC is
specifically feasible.

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:23.827
Does this make sense?

00:20:23.827 --> 00:20:25.410
Again, there's a
little bit of pattern

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:28.970
matching with definitions.

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:31.610
Most important thing
for you to understand

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:33.730
is-- and this is a big deal.

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:39.440
If the set Xf is chosen
to be a controlling

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:43.960
set for aerodynamics, then set
your MPC is perfectly feasible.

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:49.420
Now, the origin is in general
a control invariant set.

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:52.680
So it's a possible choice
for your terminal constraint.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:54.800
In general, it's
a bit suboptimal.

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:58.120
So we want to have more
sophisticated choices

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:03.320
for the terminal set within all
the possible ways of defining

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:05.120
a control invariant set.

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:07.440
We'll get back.

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:10.480
We'll get back to
this point later along

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:14.280
with a pointer to a
computational tool that

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:17.200
allows you to compute
control invariant sets.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:21.520
And in problem set 3,
you'll have a few problems

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:26.720
that will expose you to how
to use this toolbox in order

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:29.740
to compute control
invariant sets.

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:38.380
I mean, this is my
son that brings back

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:40.580
bugs from a childcare.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:44.180
This is-- the child is
a biological weapon.

00:21:44.180 --> 00:21:45.643
This is my fourth or five--

00:21:45.643 --> 00:21:46.560
I mean, I'm recording.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:48.300
So I'll stop here.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:51.660
So we're just complaining
about the diseases that

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:53.500
go around in the childcare.

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:56.540
Anyway, so the
terminal set X of f

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:59.900
is introduced artificially for
the sole purpose of leading

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:02.740
to a sufficient condition
for persistent feasibility.

00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:04.320
It's one of the tuning knobs.

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:07.760
And now we know that at least
from an abstract standpoint,

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:10.400
if we choose X of f to be
controlling variance set,

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:13.780
persistent feasibility
is guaranteed.

00:22:13.780 --> 00:22:15.780
As I said, the
simplest choice would

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:18.480
be to just pick X
of f as the origin,

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:20.180
but this is
generally undesirable

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:25.340
because it excessively restricts
the space of feasible control

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:26.580
sequences.

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:29.940
We'll discuss this choice
later in more detail.

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:34.330
Now we switch to the other topic
which is that of stability.

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:36.770
Now we know at least how to
make the problem persistently

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:37.430
feasible.

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:40.050
The tuning knob that at least
for the scope of this class

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:42.930
we choose for that
purpose is the control--

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:45.410
the terminal constraints X of f.

00:22:45.410 --> 00:22:50.010
We have other two tuning knobs,
how we select the terminal cost

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:55.050
P in our MPC formulation and
how we select the horizon N.

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:59.210
So the question now is,
can we tune those tuning

00:22:59.210 --> 00:23:02.950
knobs in such a way that
stability is guaranteed?

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:12.130
To prove stability,
we will leverage

00:23:12.130 --> 00:23:14.490
the tool of Lyapunov stability.

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:16.830
Some of you may already
be familiar with,

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:18.190
but if you are not, no problem.

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:24.650
I'll give you a very quick
intro to Lyapunov stability.

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:27.130
So what does Lyapunov
stability say?

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:31.880
I will state in a
second the full theorem,

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:34.220
but first, I want to
give you intuition.

00:23:34.220 --> 00:23:39.080
So say that you have
a dynamical system.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:46.440
SK plus 1 equal F of XK, or
maybe in continuous time,

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:50.640
you also have X
dot equal F of x.

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:55.520
Now, the question is
is the system stable?

00:23:55.520 --> 00:24:00.600
Roughly speaking, if you start
close to an equilibrium point,

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:05.080
will you go back to
that equilibrium point

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:08.900
without having trajectories
that go out of whack?

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:17.620
If this is a ball, this is
a stable equilibrium point,

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:21.600
because if you have
a ball in a ball--

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.260
I never got the
pronunciation right.

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:26.720
But anyway, so this is a
ball and this is a ball.

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:31.435
And so if you have
a ball in a ball,

00:24:31.435 --> 00:24:35.820
you know that if you start this
ball outside of the equilibrium

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:38.080
point, then it will
start oscillating.

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:40.540
And then if you have a
little bit of friction,

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:42.020
you dissipate energy.

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:46.820
You will go back to
the bottom of the ball.

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:49.540
So this is the case of a
stable equilibrium point.

00:24:49.540 --> 00:24:53.140
If you are here, of
course, this is unstable.

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:54.480
This is an equilibrium point.

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:56.940
So if you put the ball
here, it will stay here.

00:24:56.940 --> 00:24:59.940
But then if you start a little
bit farther from equilibrium

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:04.540
point, it will go down.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:07.260
So say that you have
a dynamical system

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:08.960
and say that you have
equilibrium point

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:11.380
and you want to understand
if the dynamics are

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:13.900
at least locally stable
around that equilibrium

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:17.700
point, how can you show that?

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:22.060
Well, all that you know is that
you have an equilibrium point,

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:24.140
and you have a
transition dynamics.

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:25.980
So in principle, what
you will need to do

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:29.450
is to solve for all
possible initial conditions

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:32.930
around your equilibrium
point, your discrete time

00:25:32.930 --> 00:25:35.730
equation, or
differential equation,

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:38.170
and then see what happens.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:41.170
Now, in general, solving
nonlinear differential equation

00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:47.850
or also discrete time nonlinear
update equations is complicated.

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:51.090
So for the longest time,
this was a big problem.

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:53.250
Then there was a guy,
Lyapunov, that came in

00:25:53.250 --> 00:25:55.210
and saying, well, maybe
we can say something

00:25:55.210 --> 00:25:56.610
about the stability
of the system

00:25:56.610 --> 00:25:58.970
without reasoning
directly in terms

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:03.450
of the evolution of the state.

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:08.370
The idea is if we can say that
there is some function that,

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:12.610
loosely speaking, has a notion
of an energy function, that

00:26:12.610 --> 00:26:16.770
decreases over time
along the system

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:21.570
trajectories, that could tell
me something about the evolution

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:23.010
of the underlying system.

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:26.410
Think about energy being
dissipating along the evolution

00:26:26.410 --> 00:26:27.540
of a system.

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:30.840
That means that basically
the system inherently

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:35.800
is constraint to reach a
point of minimum energy

00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:37.300
because you are
dissipating energy.

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:39.130
So basically, the
institution is if I

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:41.380
don't need to prove that all
the trajectory goes to 0.

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:44.240
If I can say, tell to the system
that the system is dissipating

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:46.760
energy, then probably,
it will converge

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:51.000
to some state of minimum
energy, loosely speaking.

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:54.720
So that's basically what
the Lyapunov theorem

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:57.800
says, considering the
equilibrium point for a system

00:26:57.800 --> 00:27:01.640
XK plus 1 equal of F of XK.

00:27:01.640 --> 00:27:05.320
Equilibrium point means
that if you are at 0, F of 0

00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:09.400
is equal to 0, meaning
that we stay there.

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:12.440
Let's consider a set omega,
which is closed-bounded

00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:14.180
and positively invariant set.

00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:16.880
This is some mathematical
thing that we

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:19.240
need, not super important.

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:23.760
Let D refer to it as
the Lyapunov function,

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:26.100
be a function that has
the following property.

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:28.260
It's a Z origin.

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:31.520
It's a positive
outside of the origin.

00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:36.380
And for the evolution
of system trajectories

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:40.380
starting from omega,
then this function

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:45.500
decreases along
the trajectories.

00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:51.380
Then X equals 0 is
asymptotically stable.

00:27:51.380 --> 00:27:54.360
So essentially, you have--

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:08.460
this is your V
function, V of X. This

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:12.220
is a two-dimensional
state space.

00:28:12.220 --> 00:28:19.660
If I can say that along all
possible system trajectories

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:25.490
V decreases in value, it means
that the state trajectory

00:28:25.490 --> 00:28:30.250
is going to the bottom, which is
the equilibrium point, since V0

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:35.170
is equal to 0, which in give
means including this notion

00:28:35.170 --> 00:28:41.530
that you are trying to show that
your system is losing energy

00:28:41.530 --> 00:28:44.480
along the system trajectory.

00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:46.730
Note how with this theorem--
that's the most important

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:47.890
thing--

00:28:47.890 --> 00:28:51.930
we can claim
stability properties

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:56.850
without having to solve
explicitly the state transition

00:28:56.850 --> 00:28:59.130
equations or a system of
differential equations

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:01.690
if we are in continuous time.

00:29:01.690 --> 00:29:04.630
So that's the main
thing about Lyapunov.

00:29:04.630 --> 00:29:08.050
As long as we can find a
function with such a property,

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:10.370
then--

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:15.130
so we can argue about stability.

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:19.290
Now, you might say, how do we
find such Lyapunov function V?

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:20.190
I mean that's hard.

00:29:20.190 --> 00:29:22.210
That's actually the hard part.

00:29:22.210 --> 00:29:30.550
So in some cases, there
are some physical arguments

00:29:30.550 --> 00:29:33.410
that allow you to construct a
Lyapunov function, for example,

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:34.990
energy arguments.

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:37.810
But in many cases, you
can use other arguments,

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:41.230
and this is the case that
will be considered for MPC.

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:44.110
Now, if you're interested
in Lyapunov theory,

00:29:44.110 --> 00:29:45.890
it has a very rich theory.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:49.310
So I would do recommend to have
a look at some of the references

00:29:49.310 --> 00:29:51.408
that I provided.

00:29:51.408 --> 00:29:53.950
For the sake of this class, the
only thing that I want to say

00:29:53.950 --> 00:29:57.110
is that there is a tool that
allows us to infer stability

00:29:57.110 --> 00:30:02.110
properties for a closed-loop
system that entails

00:30:02.110 --> 00:30:06.090
using an auxiliary function
referred to as Lyapunov function

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:10.070
and show that under the system
trajectories that Lyapunov

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:13.870
function has a non-decreasing
property, namely

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:15.688
this property here.

00:30:15.688 --> 00:30:17.230
So what we're going
to do now to show

00:30:17.230 --> 00:30:22.580
stability is to show that
for the closed-loop MPC law,

00:30:22.580 --> 00:30:26.500
we can't find a
Lyapunov function

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:28.300
so that we can then
claim stability

00:30:28.300 --> 00:30:31.980
for the MPC controller.

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:36.342
So the question is, what
is this Lyapunov function?

00:30:36.342 --> 00:30:37.800
And here, there is
another theorem.

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:40.180
What is the last one for MPC?

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:43.120
So any questions so far?

00:30:45.940 --> 00:30:48.920
How many of you heard of
Lyapunov stability before?

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:53.020
Uf!

00:30:53.020 --> 00:30:54.100
OK.

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:56.000
Well, in that case,
read about it.

00:30:56.000 --> 00:31:00.180
It's a useful and
interesting tool also with--

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:03.740
of course, I present
it here quite quickly

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:11.205
but also with a rich
physical interpretation.

00:31:11.205 --> 00:31:13.580
Again, main thing that I want
to make sure you understand

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:16.140
is that it's essentially
a tool that you

00:31:16.140 --> 00:31:17.820
have to infer
stability properties

00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:19.940
about dynamical
system without having

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:23.810
to resort to
explicit integration

00:31:23.810 --> 00:31:27.610
of the underlying
system equations.

00:31:27.610 --> 00:31:29.810
It boils down to show
that for some choice

00:31:29.810 --> 00:31:32.890
that you had to come up
with of a function V,

00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:38.850
you have a non-increasing
property of the function

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:42.130
V. If you do that, then
you can infer stability.

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:44.850
So we're going to use
that Lyapunov theorem

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:47.810
a little bit in a mechanical way
without spending additional time

00:31:47.810 --> 00:31:49.890
in explaining its
origin, but I do

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:53.490
encourage you to do a little bit
of reading on Lyapunov stability

00:31:53.490 --> 00:31:55.190
because it's an
interesting topic.

