00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:29.420
So now, I'm refining
the selection of v

00:48:29.420 --> 00:48:34.700
into the selection of a
specific v that allows me to--

00:48:34.700 --> 00:48:44.400
for AX plus Bv to stay in the
control invariant set X of f.

00:48:47.180 --> 00:48:51.420
That means that this
control sequence is now

00:48:51.420 --> 00:48:55.860
a feasible sequence for the MPC
problem starting at step one.

00:48:55.860 --> 00:48:56.380
Why?

00:48:56.380 --> 00:49:00.840
Because v satisfies the
control constraints,

00:49:00.840 --> 00:49:06.880
and the state trajectory
is a feasible in the sense

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:14.520
that all of the
states X to 0, X and 0

00:49:14.520 --> 00:49:18.520
are going to satisfy the
state constraints because

00:49:18.520 --> 00:49:22.280
of how the trajectory
of step 0 was computed.

00:49:22.280 --> 00:49:25.120
And the terminal
constraint is also

00:49:25.120 --> 00:49:28.600
satisfied because a
chosen v in such a way

00:49:28.600 --> 00:49:32.760
that the final state is in Xf.

00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:35.080
So we satisfy the
terminal constraint.

00:49:35.080 --> 00:49:39.080
So all of this to say that this
specific choice of a control

00:49:39.080 --> 00:49:44.000
sequence is feasible control
sequence for the MPC problem

00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:47.280
at step one.

00:49:47.280 --> 00:49:49.640
So now, what we're
trying to see is

00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:53.760
whether we can use this control
sequence to say something

00:49:53.760 --> 00:49:59.690
about the evolution of the
cast J0 along the trajectory.

00:49:59.690 --> 00:50:05.150
So essentially, we want
to say is a J star of X1

00:50:05.150 --> 00:50:07.070
less than J star of X0?

00:50:07.070 --> 00:50:09.252
That's what we're trying
to get to the point.

00:50:09.252 --> 00:50:10.710
Because if we can
say that, then we

00:50:10.710 --> 00:50:13.517
can invoke the Lyapunov theorem.

00:50:13.517 --> 00:50:15.350
Now, for now, the only
thing that we can say

00:50:15.350 --> 00:50:19.870
is that the J star
0 of X1 is less than

00:50:19.870 --> 00:50:29.670
or equal to the cost related to
applying this candidate control

00:50:29.670 --> 00:50:30.630
sequence.

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:34.390
Why less than or equal
to as opposed to equal?

00:50:34.390 --> 00:50:38.270
Because for now, all we know is
that this is a feasible control

00:50:38.270 --> 00:50:40.870
sequence, but it's not
necessarily optimal.

00:50:40.870 --> 00:50:42.470
So the only thing
that we can say

00:50:42.470 --> 00:50:44.950
is that the optimal
cost is upper

00:50:44.950 --> 00:50:49.590
bounded by a cost of a
feasible control sequence.

00:50:49.590 --> 00:50:51.090
OK.

00:50:54.590 --> 00:50:59.960
Now, let's add and
subtract a couple of terms.

00:50:59.960 --> 00:51:02.280
So we add the p of x and 0.

00:51:02.280 --> 00:51:03.120
We subtract it.

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:07.700
We add the C of x0,
u0, and we subtract it.

00:51:07.700 --> 00:51:12.300
Look at these
three terms in red.

00:51:12.300 --> 00:51:14.760
When you sum them,
what do you get?

00:51:18.260 --> 00:51:28.060
Remember that uk superscript
0 was the optimal control

00:51:28.060 --> 00:51:32.420
sequence for the MPC problem
from the perspective of stage 0.

00:51:32.420 --> 00:51:34.480
So when you sum those
terms, what do you get?

00:51:41.100 --> 00:51:44.720
So it's the summation
of the stage-wise cost.

00:51:44.720 --> 00:51:46.467
This is a terminal cost.

00:51:46.467 --> 00:51:48.800
So it looks like the cost of
an optimal control problem,

00:51:48.800 --> 00:51:49.300
which one?

00:51:49.300 --> 00:51:51.100
The xn0 or X of f.

00:51:51.100 --> 00:51:51.900
Exactly.

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:55.200
Plus 1 for your grade.

00:51:55.200 --> 00:51:55.700
Yes.

00:51:55.700 --> 00:51:57.240
The two and--

00:51:57.240 --> 00:51:58.340
That's exactly right.

00:51:58.340 --> 00:52:06.480
So all of these terms really
correspond to the optimal cost,

00:52:06.480 --> 00:52:10.240
because we're using the optimal
control sequence of MPC problems

00:52:10.240 --> 00:52:13.600
solved from the
perspective of step 0.

00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:16.200
So I can substitute from that.

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:19.300
So those three terms are
now encapsulated here.

00:52:19.300 --> 00:52:20.840
And then I have the
remaining terms.

00:52:24.440 --> 00:52:29.340
Now we play assumption 3,
that Lyapunov-like assumption.

00:52:32.480 --> 00:52:37.800
Assumption 3 says that
for all states in X of f,

00:52:37.800 --> 00:52:40.840
there exists a control.

00:52:40.840 --> 00:52:46.440
Let's call it v
star with a property

00:52:46.440 --> 00:52:50.000
that AX plus Bv
star is in X of f.

00:52:50.000 --> 00:52:56.870
That's what v star
does, such that--

00:52:56.870 --> 00:52:57.910
let's go back.

00:52:57.910 --> 00:52:58.890
Let me write it here.

00:52:58.890 --> 00:52:59.570
I think it's--

00:53:06.910 --> 00:53:09.410
So we keep it here.

00:53:09.410 --> 00:53:11.910
It will also be useful later.

00:53:11.910 --> 00:53:27.750
So the minimum over
u, minimum over u

00:53:27.750 --> 00:53:39.830
in u such that the Ax plus
Bu is in Xf of minus px

00:53:39.830 --> 00:53:46.290
plus a C of x, u
plus p of Ax plus Bu.

00:53:49.550 --> 00:53:55.100
All of this is less than or
equal to 0 for all X in Xf.

00:53:55.100 --> 00:53:56.600
So that's assumption 3.

00:54:03.060 --> 00:54:07.100
So now what I'm saying
is that among all those

00:54:07.100 --> 00:54:15.220
the upper bar that allow me
to transition into the control

00:54:15.220 --> 00:54:18.540
invariant set X of
f, I pick the one

00:54:18.540 --> 00:54:20.560
that minimizes this quantity.

00:54:20.560 --> 00:54:24.540
And I know that the one that
minimizes this quantity has

00:54:24.540 --> 00:54:28.620
the property that
the p of ax plus b

00:54:28.620 --> 00:54:44.700
bar minus p of x plus c of
x v bar, all of that stuff

00:54:44.700 --> 00:54:47.500
is greater than or equal to 0.

00:54:47.500 --> 00:54:53.160
So for that specific
choice of the upper bar,

00:54:53.160 --> 00:54:56.560
the three quantities in
blue sum up to something

00:54:56.560 --> 00:54:59.040
that is less than
or equal to 0, which

00:54:59.040 --> 00:55:02.680
allows me to derive the
key inequality whereby

00:55:02.680 --> 00:55:09.120
J star 0 of x1 is less than J
star 0 of x0 minus this term

00:55:09.120 --> 00:55:09.620
here.

00:55:15.480 --> 00:55:23.680
So basically, I have J star
x0 is equal to-- is less than

00:55:23.680 --> 00:55:25.760
or equal to--

00:55:25.760 --> 00:55:28.480
oops, sorry.

00:55:28.480 --> 00:55:43.140
J star of x1 is 0
plus c minus c of x0.

00:55:50.550 --> 00:55:54.150
Oh, sorry, minus c is 0.

00:55:54.150 --> 00:56:08.790
U, 0, 0 plus a term, and
then note that the term

00:56:08.790 --> 00:56:11.190
is less than or equal to 0.

00:56:11.190 --> 00:56:16.090
So that implies that the J star
x1 is less than just J star 0

00:56:16.090 --> 00:56:18.310
x0 minus this quantity here.

00:56:22.710 --> 00:56:23.610
Does it make sense?

00:56:26.190 --> 00:56:29.070
Please, this term by
assumption 3, I know

00:56:29.070 --> 00:56:31.270
is less than or equal to 0.

00:56:31.270 --> 00:56:35.110
Well, but c of x0
u0 is greater than 0

00:56:35.110 --> 00:56:38.250
for all initial states that
are not the origin, again,

00:56:38.250 --> 00:56:42.230
because the cost is a
positive for all states that

00:56:42.230 --> 00:56:44.790
are not equal to the
origin because of--

00:56:44.790 --> 00:56:46.550
is a quadratic cost.

00:56:46.550 --> 00:56:52.940
So we have just shown that
the J star x1 is strictly

00:56:52.940 --> 00:56:57.860
less than the J star x0,
meaning that the cost is indeed

00:56:57.860 --> 00:57:01.660
guaranteed to decrease along
the system trajectories.

00:57:01.660 --> 00:57:05.400
So then, we can invoke the
Lyapunov stability theory,

00:57:05.400 --> 00:57:09.780
and then claim that indeed,
the MPC control law leads

00:57:09.780 --> 00:57:12.407
to closed-loop dynamics that
are asymptotically stable,

00:57:12.407 --> 00:57:14.240
so are guaranteed to
converge to the origin,

00:57:14.240 --> 00:57:16.620
assuming the assumptions
are verified with domain

00:57:16.620 --> 00:57:20.820
of attraction x and 0.

00:57:20.820 --> 00:57:22.815
Any questions here?

00:57:22.815 --> 00:57:25.980
Is it an interesting proof?

00:57:25.980 --> 00:57:27.480
I think that's an
interesting proof.

00:57:27.480 --> 00:57:29.060
It's actually quite powerful.

00:57:29.060 --> 00:57:32.580
So try to go through it a few
times just to internalize it.

00:57:32.580 --> 00:57:35.760
This is a very important proof.

00:57:35.760 --> 00:57:36.260
OK.

00:57:36.260 --> 00:57:38.500
So now, we have a
theorem that allow

00:57:38.500 --> 00:57:41.380
us to reason in terms of
persistent feasibility,

00:57:41.380 --> 00:57:44.540
a theorem that allow us to
reason in terms of stability.

00:57:44.540 --> 00:57:46.600
How do we make those
theorems actionable?

00:57:46.600 --> 00:57:48.840
Meaning how do we
link those theorems

00:57:48.840 --> 00:57:53.600
to specific selections for x
of f and the terminal cost?

00:57:53.600 --> 00:57:57.280
Which in our case, we assume
the terminal cost is quadratic.

00:57:57.280 --> 00:58:01.200
So we want to understand
what this theorem tells us

00:58:01.200 --> 00:58:05.067
in terms of how we
choose a capital P.

00:58:05.067 --> 00:58:06.900
And we're going to see
a couple of settings.

00:58:06.900 --> 00:58:10.580
So the first setting is
we're given linear dynamics.

00:58:10.580 --> 00:58:13.000
But now, A is
asymptotically stable,

00:58:13.000 --> 00:58:20.000
meaning that all eigenvalues of
A lie within the unit circle.

00:58:20.000 --> 00:58:26.480
So let's define Xf as the
maximally positive invariant

00:58:26.480 --> 00:58:30.520
set for the system xt
plus 1 equals A of xt.

00:58:30.520 --> 00:58:33.260
How do we compute this
positive invariant set?

00:58:33.260 --> 00:58:35.200
I'll show you in
a couple of slides

00:58:35.200 --> 00:58:39.720
the link to a very handy
toolbox to compute these sets

00:58:39.720 --> 00:58:41.200
numerically.

00:58:41.200 --> 00:58:43.560
So you just use
this Matlab toolbox,

00:58:43.560 --> 00:58:48.270
write down a couple of commands,
and you will get this set.

00:58:48.270 --> 00:58:53.030
Now, X of f as defined
is a control invariant

00:58:53.030 --> 00:58:58.550
set since once you
are in this set,

00:58:58.550 --> 00:59:04.030
since u equals 0 is a feasible
set, then A of 0 is 0.

00:59:04.030 --> 00:59:05.210
So you stay there.

00:59:14.030 --> 00:59:16.190
So the persistent
feasibility part

00:59:16.190 --> 00:59:18.130
is relatively straightforward.

00:59:18.130 --> 00:59:19.550
Now, how do we prove--

00:59:19.550 --> 00:59:22.750
actually, how do we use
the stability theorem?

00:59:22.750 --> 00:59:28.350
Remember what the stability
theorem is asking us.

00:59:28.350 --> 00:59:31.930
Actually, let me write it in
terms of a quadratic cost.

00:59:41.190 --> 00:59:50.660
So minus x transpose Px plus x
transpose qx plus u transpose Ru

00:59:50.660 --> 01:00:00.380
plus Ax plus Bu
transpose P Ax plus Bu.

01:00:00.380 --> 01:00:03.980
So we want this stuff to
be less than or equal to 0

01:00:03.980 --> 01:00:05.880
for all x in Xf.

01:00:13.140 --> 01:00:18.340
So let's consider the
specific control u equal to 0.

01:00:18.340 --> 01:00:21.460
The question is for
this specific control u,

01:00:21.460 --> 01:00:23.740
is this inequality satisfied?

01:00:23.740 --> 01:00:25.360
If that is the
case, that's great.

01:00:25.360 --> 01:00:31.580
Then we can [CLEARS THROAT]
infer stability.

01:00:31.580 --> 01:00:36.940
Now, if you set u equal
to 0, this term goes away.

01:00:36.940 --> 01:00:38.660
It's equal to 0.

01:00:38.660 --> 01:00:39.720
This term goes away.

01:00:39.720 --> 01:00:40.760
This term goes away.

01:00:40.760 --> 01:00:45.360
So essentially, we are left with
the inequality minus x transpose

01:00:45.360 --> 01:00:52.200
Px plus x transpose
Qx plus x transpose,

01:00:52.200 --> 01:00:58.680
A transpose PAx less
than or equal to 0.

01:00:58.680 --> 01:01:02.980
It just so happens that this is
not a random matrix equation.

01:01:02.980 --> 01:01:05.080
It's actually a very
famous equation referred to

01:01:05.080 --> 01:01:07.400
as the Lyapunov equation.

01:01:07.400 --> 01:01:14.520
And it turns out that if Q is
positive definite and IA is

01:01:14.520 --> 01:01:18.760
a statically stable, there
exists a positive definite P

01:01:18.760 --> 01:01:22.920
such that all this quantity
is equal to 0, which

01:01:22.920 --> 01:01:25.920
satisfies this condition here.

01:01:25.920 --> 01:01:26.960
So what does that mean?

01:01:26.960 --> 01:01:30.600
That if you choose
P as the solution

01:01:30.600 --> 01:01:32.160
to the Lyapunov
equation, you are

01:01:32.160 --> 01:01:35.020
guaranteed that such a solution
exists and is positive definite.

01:01:35.020 --> 01:01:37.720
So it satisfy all our
stability assumptions.

01:01:37.720 --> 01:01:41.080
With that selection of
P as a terminal cost,

01:01:41.080 --> 01:01:46.590
MPC is going to be
asymptotically stable.

01:01:46.590 --> 01:01:50.950
So what's the intuition here?

01:01:50.950 --> 01:01:55.230
The intuition here is
that basically, you

01:01:55.230 --> 01:02:03.350
optimize MPC in such a way
that after n steps, you end up,

01:02:03.350 --> 01:02:11.630
if you do nothing afterwards,
into these all infinity set.

01:02:11.630 --> 01:02:13.430
And then you will let
your natural dynamics

01:02:13.430 --> 01:02:16.470
that are stable to
drive you to the origin.

01:02:16.470 --> 01:02:19.750
In reality, you won't do that,
because by re-optimizing,

01:02:19.750 --> 01:02:24.050
you're always going to find
some different control sequence.

01:02:24.050 --> 01:02:27.070
But basically, that
set serves as an anchor

01:02:27.070 --> 01:02:31.670
to make sure that
you stay grounded

01:02:31.670 --> 01:02:34.150
into desirable behavior.

01:02:34.150 --> 01:02:35.810
Mechanically, what do you do?

01:02:35.810 --> 01:02:37.510
You call a computational
tool, that I'm

01:02:37.510 --> 01:02:40.030
going to mention in
a second, to compute

01:02:40.030 --> 01:02:42.200
such a maximal
positive invariant set,

01:02:42.200 --> 01:02:45.780
and then we choose a P in order
to solve the Lyapunov equation.

01:02:45.780 --> 01:02:48.260
It's another one-line
command in Matlab.

01:02:48.260 --> 01:02:50.028
You plug those
numbers into your MPC.

01:02:50.028 --> 01:02:52.320
You're guaranteed persistent
feasibility and stability.

01:02:56.700 --> 01:03:06.723
So what if A is not stable?

01:03:06.723 --> 01:03:08.140
Meaning that your
natural dynamics

01:03:08.140 --> 01:03:11.117
don't help you with going to
the origin without control.

01:03:11.117 --> 01:03:13.700
What that means is that you have
to do active control in order

01:03:13.700 --> 01:03:16.140
to make sure that
you go to the origin.

01:03:16.140 --> 01:03:22.100
So in this case, you still have
a similar procedure in order

01:03:22.100 --> 01:03:26.860
to tune your parameters.

01:03:26.860 --> 01:03:31.060
But you now use
LQR active control

01:03:31.060 --> 01:03:35.140
as basically your
controller that

01:03:35.140 --> 01:03:39.600
allows you to anchor your future
dynamics into some dynamics that

01:03:39.600 --> 01:03:41.520
will lead you to the origin.

01:03:41.520 --> 01:03:45.040
Specifically, let f
infinity be the optimal gain

01:03:45.040 --> 01:03:47.680
for the infinite
horizon LQR controller.

01:03:47.680 --> 01:03:51.440
Set X of f as the maximum
positive invariant set

01:03:51.440 --> 01:03:55.580
with respect to the LQR
closed-loop dynamics,

01:03:55.580 --> 01:04:03.800
which means that after n
steps, when you hit set X of f,

01:04:03.800 --> 01:04:05.220
if X of f.

01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:07.600
If you don't do any
replanning, at least

01:04:07.600 --> 01:04:10.880
you have a feasible LQR
controller that allows you

01:04:10.880 --> 01:04:15.160
to stay within a set X of f.

01:04:15.160 --> 01:04:19.200
But then, if you choose
the terminal cost

01:04:19.200 --> 01:04:24.240
p in such a way that
your LQR controller

01:04:24.240 --> 01:04:26.640
which essentially brings
you to the origin,

01:04:26.640 --> 01:04:29.445
then you are guaranteed
to be stable.

01:04:29.445 --> 01:04:31.320
Now, here the derivation
is a little bit more

01:04:31.320 --> 01:04:32.820
convoluted algebraically.

01:04:32.820 --> 01:04:35.000
So I'm not going to show it.

01:04:35.000 --> 01:04:38.850
But essentially, the style of
reasoning would be the same.

01:04:38.850 --> 01:04:44.470
We plug the controller, the LQR
controller, u equal f infinity x

01:04:44.470 --> 01:04:46.550
into this inequality.

01:04:46.550 --> 01:04:51.670
And then we will show
that if we pick p

01:04:51.670 --> 01:04:54.910
as the solution of
this equation, then

01:04:54.910 --> 01:04:56.090
we will have equality.

01:04:56.090 --> 01:04:59.750
So we will satisfy the
stability condition.

01:04:59.750 --> 01:05:03.010
And this equation, again,
is not a random equation.

01:05:03.010 --> 01:05:06.030
This is the Riccati
equation, and it's

01:05:06.030 --> 01:05:08.310
guaranteed to have a solution.

01:05:08.310 --> 01:05:12.550
So bottom line, if you
have an unstable system,

01:05:12.550 --> 01:05:16.910
you pick your terminal set as
the maximum positive invariant

01:05:16.910 --> 01:05:20.950
set for the closed-loop
LQR dynamics.

01:05:20.950 --> 01:05:24.410
You pick p as a solution to the
discrete time Riccati equation.

01:05:24.410 --> 01:05:26.410
You plug those numbers
into your MPC controller,

01:05:26.410 --> 01:05:28.785
and then you're guaranteed to
have persistent feasibility

01:05:28.785 --> 01:05:29.430
and stability.

01:05:29.430 --> 01:05:33.430
And you will have a couple
of problems in your present.

01:05:33.430 --> 01:05:35.970
Now, these are not necessarily
the best possible choices,

01:05:35.970 --> 01:05:40.060
but just some choices
are very procedural.

01:05:40.060 --> 01:05:44.500
So if A is stable maximally
positive invariant set

01:05:44.500 --> 01:05:46.980
with respect to the
uncontrolled dynamics,

01:05:46.980 --> 01:05:49.900
choose p as the solution
to Lyapunov equation.

01:05:49.900 --> 01:05:54.140
A is unstable,
choose your terminal

01:05:54.140 --> 01:05:59.320
set as the positive invariant
set for your LQR closed-loop

01:05:59.320 --> 01:05:59.820
dynamics.

01:05:59.820 --> 01:06:07.777
And choose p as a solution
to the Riccati equation,

01:06:07.777 --> 01:06:09.860
with the idea, again, that
you basically carve out

01:06:09.860 --> 01:06:13.660
a region X of f where the
optimal unconstrained controller

01:06:13.660 --> 01:06:15.540
is constrained to
be feasible, and it

01:06:15.540 --> 01:06:19.120
is used as a value function
to guarantee stability.

01:06:22.460 --> 01:06:22.960
Well.

01:06:22.960 --> 01:06:24.740
That was quite a bit.

01:06:24.740 --> 01:06:29.340
Let me conclude with
two very quick things.

01:06:29.340 --> 01:06:31.980
So the first one is
that so far, we've

01:06:31.980 --> 01:06:36.060
been looking at MPC through the
lens of online optimization.

01:06:36.060 --> 01:06:39.620
So in measuring your state, you
solving an optimization problem

01:06:39.620 --> 01:06:41.560
online, basically
on your device.

01:06:41.560 --> 01:06:42.700
Get the control sequence.

01:06:42.700 --> 01:06:46.520
You apply the first control
input, and then you reoptimize.

01:06:46.520 --> 01:06:51.960
Now, it turns out that there
is also an explicit offline

01:06:51.960 --> 01:06:58.040
version of MPC that allow you to
pre-compute MPC controller such

01:06:58.040 --> 01:07:03.360
that you don't have to do
any optimization online.

01:07:03.360 --> 01:07:07.360
To appreciate that, let's
go back to our usual setup.

01:07:07.360 --> 01:07:09.200
By the way, MPC can
be generalized also

01:07:09.200 --> 01:07:12.080
to nonlinear systems.

01:07:12.080 --> 01:07:14.240
But here, the goal
of this class is

01:07:14.240 --> 01:07:16.820
to give a broad overview of
different control techniques.

01:07:16.820 --> 01:07:19.445
So we'll only discuss it in the
context of linear system, which

01:07:19.445 --> 01:07:21.000
is already quite interesting.

01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:21.920
So we're back to--

01:07:21.920 --> 01:07:26.360
I mean, usual setup linear
dynamics quadratic cost.

01:07:26.360 --> 01:07:31.800
So it turns out that the
solution to the constraint LQR

01:07:31.800 --> 01:07:36.070
problem is a control, which is
a continuous piecewise affine

01:07:36.070 --> 01:07:39.230
function and a polyhedral
partition of the state space

01:07:39.230 --> 01:07:42.190
that is--

01:07:42.190 --> 01:07:44.450
There exists a partition,
the state space,

01:07:44.450 --> 01:07:50.710
so that if say, in 1,
2, 3, 4, 5 regions such

01:07:50.710 --> 01:07:57.110
that if your initial state for
your MPC computation is here,

01:07:57.110 --> 01:08:00.010
you can just look
at a lookup table.

01:08:00.010 --> 01:08:04.270
And then the optimal control
policy is simply F of x plus g,

01:08:04.270 --> 01:08:09.550
where the F, K, J and G,
K, J are gains associated

01:08:09.550 --> 01:08:11.110
with this specific region.

01:08:11.110 --> 01:08:13.790
So basically, the
solution to LQR

01:08:13.790 --> 01:08:22.750
is just a piecewise
affine control,

01:08:22.750 --> 01:08:27.630
which allows you to basically
do a lookup table search online,

01:08:27.630 --> 01:08:29.990
meaning that you
measure the state.

01:08:29.990 --> 01:08:33.689
You see in which
region the state falls.

01:08:33.689 --> 01:08:38.998
You pull out the gains
corresponding to that region,

01:08:38.998 --> 01:08:40.790
and then you have your
optimal control law.

01:08:40.790 --> 01:08:42.957
There is no optimization
that you have to do online.

01:08:46.770 --> 01:08:50.649
And these terms F and G and the
partition of the state space

01:08:50.649 --> 01:08:52.694
is all computed offline.

01:08:52.694 --> 01:08:54.069
I'm not telling
you how to do it.

01:08:54.069 --> 01:08:56.729
I'm just saying that
this can be done.

01:08:56.729 --> 01:08:58.109
So you say, great.

01:08:58.109 --> 01:09:01.370
Why don't you do
it all the time?

01:09:01.370 --> 01:09:03.310
I mean, this is great
for a number of reasons.

01:09:03.310 --> 01:09:06.250
Basically, it avoids you to
solve an optimization problem

01:09:06.250 --> 01:09:07.410
online.

01:09:07.410 --> 01:09:09.090
Solving optimization
problem also

01:09:09.090 --> 01:09:13.010
has its own challenges beyond
just how quickly an optimization

01:09:13.010 --> 01:09:13.990
problem can be solved.

01:09:13.990 --> 01:09:16.890
For example, you have
to show that you are

01:09:16.890 --> 01:09:18.670
in a safety critical situation.

01:09:18.670 --> 01:09:20.885
You want to land
a rocket on Earth.

01:09:20.885 --> 01:09:23.010
Well, it means that you
really trust your optimizer

01:09:23.010 --> 01:09:24.250
not to have bugs.

01:09:24.250 --> 01:09:27.312
So from a development problem
standpoint, it has costs.

01:09:27.312 --> 01:09:28.770
You need to have
additional people.

01:09:28.770 --> 01:09:30.450
They had the right optimizer.

01:09:30.450 --> 01:09:31.710
They had to customize it.

01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:34.220
They had to code it into
an embedded environment.

01:09:34.220 --> 01:09:35.680
You had to validate it.

01:09:35.680 --> 01:09:37.140
Here, everything is explicit.

01:09:37.140 --> 01:09:40.240
So the validation
step is much easier.

01:09:40.240 --> 01:09:43.319
On the other hand, it
turns out that oftentimes,

01:09:43.319 --> 01:09:45.560
these polyhedral
partitions of the space

01:09:45.560 --> 01:09:48.160
takes very weird shapes.

01:09:48.160 --> 01:09:51.399
I mean, meaning that
you have a lot of cells.

01:09:51.399 --> 01:09:55.360
And today, the optimizer,
such as the convex optimizers

01:09:55.360 --> 01:09:58.080
have become so good
that oftentimes, it's

01:09:58.080 --> 01:10:00.280
faster to just solve
the optimization problem

01:10:00.280 --> 01:10:04.200
rather than just doing a lookup
table in a gigantic table.

01:10:04.200 --> 01:10:09.760
So explicit MPC has a
good concept for you

01:10:09.760 --> 01:10:13.040
to know with understanding
that oftentimes, you just use

01:10:13.040 --> 01:10:14.740
online optimization these days.

01:10:17.720 --> 01:10:25.720
So we essentially discussed the
famous multi-step design process

01:10:25.720 --> 01:10:28.620
for MPC where we choose.

01:10:28.620 --> 01:10:31.170
You can also play a little
bit with the horizon length.

01:10:31.170 --> 01:10:33.630
In this class, we
focus primarily on Xf

01:10:33.630 --> 01:10:37.450
as your tuning knob for
persistent feasibility.

01:10:37.450 --> 01:10:40.270
So you tune Xf to be controlled
environment so that you

01:10:40.270 --> 01:10:41.830
have persistent feasibility.

01:10:41.830 --> 01:10:44.030
You choose the
parameters Q and R

01:10:44.030 --> 01:10:46.310
freely to affect the
control performance.

01:10:46.310 --> 01:10:49.330
You adjust capital P as
per the stability theorem,

01:10:49.330 --> 01:10:53.230
and then you have an automated
way to tune your MPC algorithm.

01:10:53.230 --> 01:10:56.830
To compute these
control invariant

01:10:56.830 --> 01:11:00.670
sets and so on, this is a very
handy toolbox, which is MT3,

01:11:00.670 --> 01:11:04.570
and that's what you will
work with in your P set 3.

01:11:08.710 --> 01:11:12.990
One last thing in the
last four minutes--

01:11:12.990 --> 01:11:16.910
so far, we have looked
at MPC as a tool in order

01:11:16.910 --> 01:11:19.773
to achieve stability.

01:11:19.773 --> 01:11:21.190
So basically, we
want to make sure

01:11:21.190 --> 01:11:23.070
that the state converges to 0.

01:11:23.070 --> 01:11:27.810
But oftentimes, you want
to use MPC for tracking.

01:11:27.810 --> 01:11:30.090
So basically, you're giving
a reference trajectory,

01:11:30.090 --> 01:11:32.570
for example, computed
through a direct method,

01:11:32.570 --> 01:11:35.530
and then you want to use an MPC
wrapper that basically allows

01:11:35.530 --> 01:11:39.010
you to counteract
any disturbances

01:11:39.010 --> 01:11:41.250
and make sure that your
actual trajectory stays

01:11:41.250 --> 01:11:44.770
close to the
reference trajectory.

01:11:44.770 --> 01:11:49.130
So the most naive way of
setting up the tracking problem

01:11:49.130 --> 01:11:51.970
is to have a cost where
you penalize deviations

01:11:51.970 --> 01:11:56.890
from your reference trajectory
plus a quadratic penalty

01:11:56.890 --> 01:11:58.970
for the control effort.

01:11:58.970 --> 01:12:01.890
So this formulation
does not work.

01:12:01.890 --> 01:12:07.690
The reason why it doesn't
work is that oftentimes,

01:12:07.690 --> 01:12:12.570
to track a reference trajectory,
you need a non-zero control.

01:12:12.570 --> 01:12:15.970
So say that you have an aircraft
and you want to track a constant

01:12:15.970 --> 01:12:19.190
altitude profile, you cannot
switch off the engine.

01:12:19.190 --> 01:12:21.850
You still need a
constant thrust.

01:12:21.850 --> 01:12:24.050
So what happens if you
use this formulation is

01:12:24.050 --> 01:12:29.920
that at the moment your
tracking error goes to 0,

01:12:29.920 --> 01:12:32.300
now the cost is dominated
by your control effort.

01:12:32.300 --> 01:12:35.020
So now, MPC is trying to
minimize the control effort.

01:12:35.020 --> 01:12:39.640
So basically, you reduce thrust,
and then your tracking cost

01:12:39.640 --> 01:12:40.140
goes up.

01:12:40.140 --> 01:12:42.098
So you start going to
this kind of oscillations

01:12:42.098 --> 01:12:47.600
in between control effort
and tracking error cost.

01:12:47.600 --> 01:12:49.520
So what's the solution?

01:12:49.520 --> 01:12:53.980
You reframe the problem in
terms of delta controls.

01:12:53.980 --> 01:12:58.440
So basically, how you change
controls with respect to time

01:12:58.440 --> 01:13:00.460
steps as opposed to
absolute control.

01:13:00.460 --> 01:13:03.240
So you frame the
tracking problem

01:13:03.240 --> 01:13:06.860
as a problem of
minimizing tracking error.

01:13:06.860 --> 01:13:10.320
So y is C of xx.

01:13:10.320 --> 01:13:14.240
I mean, C could be also the
identity, could be just xk

01:13:14.240 --> 01:13:19.360
minus rk, where r is your
reference, plus a penalty

01:13:19.360 --> 01:13:23.000
term with respect to
the deviation control

01:13:23.000 --> 01:13:25.640
variables, where the
delta Us are defined

01:13:25.640 --> 01:13:29.150
as the uk minus uk minus 1.

01:13:29.150 --> 01:13:33.870
So that in perfect tracking
what you have is a 0 tracking

01:13:33.870 --> 01:13:38.630
error and a constant control,
meaning constant thrust, which

01:13:38.630 --> 01:13:40.550
means delta u equals
0, which corresponds

01:13:40.550 --> 01:13:45.230
to what MPC really wants
to do in this formulation.

01:13:45.230 --> 01:13:48.573
So all of this to say that
this is a better version,

01:13:48.573 --> 01:13:49.990
and there are other
considerations

01:13:49.990 --> 01:13:51.407
to take into account
for tracking.

01:13:51.407 --> 01:13:54.550
But just to give you some
intuition about how for tracking

01:13:54.550 --> 01:13:56.710
want to be a bit
more sophisticated

01:13:56.710 --> 01:13:58.470
in how you set up MPC problem.

01:13:58.470 --> 01:14:01.030
In particular, typically,
you want to use reason

01:14:01.030 --> 01:14:03.930
in terms of delta controls.

01:14:03.930 --> 01:14:06.270
So the optimization
variables that are delta

01:14:06.270 --> 01:14:09.710
use that are defined
as uk minus uk minus 1,

01:14:09.710 --> 01:14:13.490
you are given the u
at time t minus 1,

01:14:13.490 --> 01:14:15.950
and then you obtain
all the Us recursively

01:14:15.950 --> 01:14:21.070
by adding the delta Us that
are really the optimization

01:14:21.070 --> 01:14:21.615
variables.

01:14:21.615 --> 01:14:22.490
Does that make sense?

01:14:28.130 --> 01:14:28.710
All right.

01:14:28.710 --> 01:14:30.150
So we did quite a bit.

01:14:30.150 --> 01:14:33.650
So this lecture
essentially wraps up

01:14:33.650 --> 01:14:35.090
the first part of
the class, where

01:14:35.090 --> 01:14:39.450
we assume that everything
about the model is known.

01:14:39.450 --> 01:14:41.450
Starting from next
week, we'll go

01:14:41.450 --> 01:14:47.570
into the new setting,
whereby the model may not

01:14:47.570 --> 01:14:49.670
be completely known anymore.

01:14:49.670 --> 01:14:53.050
So either, you have to adapt
to the part of the model that

01:14:53.050 --> 01:14:57.050
is unknown or learn
what you do not

01:14:57.050 --> 01:15:00.170
know in order to still act
optimally or very close

01:15:00.170 --> 01:15:02.370
to optimally.

01:15:02.370 --> 01:15:04.150
So P3 will be an
interesting one.

01:15:04.150 --> 01:15:08.010
So it will expose you to
a lot of these MPC ideas

01:15:08.010 --> 01:15:15.810
and will also force you to work
with the MPT3 toolbox, which

01:15:15.810 --> 01:15:18.170
is kind of neat.

01:15:18.170 --> 01:15:18.750
All right.

01:15:18.750 --> 01:15:21.080
So see you next week.

